  "worked_solution": "The Hadamard product of two matrices (or vectors) is only defined if they have the same dimensions.\n\nFirst, notice that $X$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $Y$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $u$ is a $3\\times 1$ column vector.\n\nNow, let's consider each expression in turn:\n\n- The dimensions of $YX$ are $2\\times 2,$ and the dimensions of $Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)$ are $2\\times 3.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression I is *not* well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $X$ are $3\\times 2,$ and the dimensions of $Yu$ are $2\\times 1.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression II is *not* well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)$ are $2\\times 3,$ and the dimensions of $YX{X}^{T}$ are $2\\times 3.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression III is well-defined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\".",
